id: rust.error-enum
kind: rust
title: Custom error enums
aliases: error enum, domain errors
tags: errors, api design
questions:
  - how do you design an error type for a library?
invariant: Each variant encodes a distinct caller decision, so callers can match instead of parsing messages.
answer_30s: Define an enum with one variant per distinguishable failure and derive thiserror::Error, so callers choose a response from a variant rather than from text.
answer_2m: |
  The test is decision-making: if two failures lead to the same caller action
  they can share a variant; if one means 404 and the other means alert, they
  must be separate. Keep the source chain (`#[source]`/`#[from]`), keep
  implementation types (a raw reqwest::Error) out of the public boundary, and
  expose enough structure that the HTTP layer maps variants to status codes
  without string matching. In an application, anyhow at the top level is fine;
  at a library or protocol boundary, precise enums are the contract.
when_to_use:
  - public library APIs, protocol boundaries, anything with retry or status semantics
avoid_when:
  - internal glue where one opaque error is genuinely enough
minimal_example: |
  #[derive(Debug, thiserror::Error)]
  enum ApiError {
      #[error("not found: {0}")] NotFound(String),
      #[error("invalid input: {reason}")] Invalid { reason: String },
      #[error("upstream failed")] Upstream(#[from] reqwest::Error),
  }

  fn status(e: &ApiError) -> u16 {
      match e {
          ApiError::NotFound(_) => 404,
          ApiError::Invalid { .. } => 400,
          ApiError::Upstream(_) => 502,
      }
  }
failure_modes:
  - one catch-all Other(String), forcing callers to match on message text
  - leaking implementation errors across the API boundary
related:
  - rust.result
  - rust.anyhow-thiserror
  - rust.question-mark
sources:
  - https://docs.rs/thiserror/latest/thiserror/
